        {
            "source_entity_instance": "krill predator prey model",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SOURCE_COMPONENT",
            "target_entity_instance": "predation model",
            "target_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "paragraph": "Antarctic krill is preyed on by a variety of predators, such as seals, seabirds, baleen whales, fishes and squid. The predation by mammals and birds is generally better understood than that of fishes and squid (Trahan & Hill, 2016). Due to uncertainties of krill predation by fishes and squid (see discussion) and our focus on the detailed dynamics of the krill populations, we represented predators as a single population. The predator population increases as a function of the size of the krill population and decays with a time constant of two years.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "krill predator prey model",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SOURCE_COMPONENT",
            "target_entity_instance": "fishing model",
            "target_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "paragraph": "Krill trawling captures krill animals over a range of sizes, dependent on fishing gear design, including differences in mesh sizes used (Wang et al., 2021). An observational study of krill sizes captured by two different trawlers found averages between 50.8 \u00b1 3.7 mm and 36.3 \u00b1 5.4 mm, a significant range (Wang, 2016). We therefore modeled krill fishing as a size-specific removal of krill biomass, with a 30% increase in the likelihood of fishing captures for every 40 mm of krill, in 10% of fishing events.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "krill predator prey model",
